1
00:00:02,251 --> 00:00:04,712
'"I want you to take out
your list of Red John suspects"

2
00:00:04,837 --> 00:00:05,922
"and look at the names."

3
00:00:06,047 --> 00:00:07,256
"I'll give you a moment."

4
00:00:09,842 --> 00:00:10,885
"The names are..."

5
00:00:11,135 --> 00:00:12,345
{\an8}"Bret Stiles"

6
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:15,848
{\an8}"Gale Bertram"

7
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:18,935
{\an8}"Raymond Haffner"

8
00:00:19,769 --> 00:00:20,895
{\an8}"Reede Smith"

9
00:00:22,355 --> 00:00:23,773
{\an8}"Bob Kirkland"

10
00:00:24,982 --> 00:00:27,276
{\an8}"Sheriff Thomas McAllister"

11
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:28,986
{\an8}"Brett Partridge."

12
00:00:30,655 --> 00:00:31,697
"There."

13
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:36,285
"I'm going to start killing again.
Often."

14
00:00:36,786 --> 00:00:38,287
"Until you catch me..."

15
00:00:39,288 --> 00:00:40,706
"or I catch you."

16
00:01:12,405 --> 00:01:13,948
- How did he do that?
- Huh?

17
00:01:14,699 --> 00:01:16,993
That tape was made
two months ago.

18
00:01:18,035 --> 00:01:20,997
Red John knew the names on the list
of suspects before you did.

19
00:01:21,706 --> 00:01:23,207
I mean, how is that even possible?

20
00:01:23,874 --> 00:01:25,126
Eh, it's a trick.

21
00:01:25,459 --> 00:01:26,502
A trick how?

22
00:01:27,044 --> 00:01:28,087
I don't know.

23
00:01:35,386 --> 00:01:36,429
Lisbon.

24
00:01:37,388 --> 00:01:38,431
Chief.

25
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:40,891
I'll be right there.

26
00:01:43,936 --> 00:01:46,606
That was Bertram.
We have a case.

27
00:01:46,731 --> 00:01:47,773
Huh?

28
00:01:48,024 --> 00:01:50,067
- We have a case.
- Now?

29
00:01:51,110 --> 00:01:52,612
Uh, I don't wanna work
right now, Lisbon.

30
00:01:52,737 --> 00:01:53,779
I need to think.

31
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:57,158
We need to carry on as normal.
We don't wanna look freaked out.

32
00:01:59,619 --> 00:02:00,786
I'm not freaked out.

33
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:17,595
- Yeah. Hi, guys.
- Hey, Sarge.

34
00:02:18,012 --> 00:02:19,513
- Good to see you again.
- Nice to see you.

35
00:02:20,348 --> 00:02:22,600
- How are things?
- It's been pretty busy.

36
00:02:23,017 --> 00:02:24,060
Hey there.

37
00:02:25,144 --> 00:02:27,855
- He can't say hello?
- Eh, it's a tough day.

38
00:02:28,648 --> 00:02:31,317
So I understand you got the call
about three hours ago.

39
00:02:31,734 --> 00:02:32,777
What do we got?

40
00:02:33,235 --> 00:02:35,279
The victim's name
is Sandra Helvig.

41
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:37,573
She lives here with her fiancé.

42
00:02:38,032 --> 00:02:40,409
That's Cody Benbow,
the baseball player.

43
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:44,080
"So he comes home from practice
and finds her lying right here."

44
00:02:44,246 --> 00:02:46,332
"Looks like she's strangled
with her own scarf."

45
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:49,293
"Probably dead for a couple of hours.
We're waiting for the coroner."

46
00:03:28,541 --> 00:03:29,959
The back window is broken

47
00:03:30,084 --> 00:03:31,919
There's both jewelry
and cash missing.

48
00:03:32,086 --> 00:03:34,672
We're thinking she came home
and surprised the burglar.

49
00:03:35,965 --> 00:03:40,219
Well, besides the forced entry, I don't see
any obvious signs of a struggle here.

50
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:44,181
Hey, Cody, how much was stolen?
Dollar value?

51
00:03:44,473 --> 00:03:47,852
I don't know, sir. Her engagement ring
alone was 50 grand. Um...

52
00:03:48,728 --> 00:03:50,146
A couple hundred grand in total.

53
00:03:50,312 --> 00:03:51,772
What's your batting average
this year?

54
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:53,023
Two sixty.

55
00:03:53,482 --> 00:03:56,026
- What about last year?
- Three twelve.

56
00:03:56,610 --> 00:03:57,737
Oh. What happened?

57
00:03:58,612 --> 00:04:00,573
You came here
to talk about baseball?

58
00:04:02,450 --> 00:04:04,285
Yeah, I'm trying to work here
and you're staring at me

59
00:04:04,410 --> 00:04:05,661
like I have two heads.

60
00:04:06,787 --> 00:04:08,164
Do I look like
I have two heads?

61
00:04:08,330 --> 00:04:10,291
- Uh, no.
- No.

62
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:13,753
Sarge, why don't you and I go
outside, do our paperwork?

63
00:04:15,755 --> 00:04:16,797
Cody, look at me.

64
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:21,469
I think it was you
that killed Sandra.

65
00:04:22,178 --> 00:04:23,888
Sir, you are flat-out wrong.

66
00:04:24,472 --> 00:04:26,849
She is the love of my life.
Why would I kill her?

67
00:04:26,974 --> 00:04:28,017
You tell me.

68
00:04:28,476 --> 00:04:30,770
There is no reason
in the world, sir.

69
00:04:30,895 --> 00:04:31,979
No reason.

70
00:04:32,688 --> 00:04:34,106
Okay, Cody. Fair enough.

71
00:04:34,523 --> 00:04:36,358
I know when a man
is telling me the truth.

72
00:04:36,650 --> 00:04:38,861
- Do you have any guns?
- Do I have any guns?

73
00:04:38,986 --> 00:04:41,405
- Yeah.
- Y-yeah, yeah, I-I do.

74
00:04:41,572 --> 00:04:42,698
Where? What kind?

75
00:04:42,823 --> 00:04:45,034
- A couple of handguns or so in the safe.
- Show me.

76
00:04:45,159 --> 00:04:46,744
What does guns
have to do with anything?

77
00:04:46,869 --> 00:04:48,996
I'm sorry, but she was strangled.
Can you not see that?

78
00:04:49,121 --> 00:04:51,207
Who's in charge here,
me or you?

79
00:04:51,999 --> 00:04:53,918
Um, I just wanna see your guns.

80
00:04:54,084 --> 00:04:56,712
I can search this place
from the roof to the basement.

81
00:04:57,004 --> 00:04:59,799
- I can dig up the garden if I want to.
- O-okay, all right.

82
00:04:59,924 --> 00:05:01,926
Just, just chill.
I'll show you my guns.

83
00:05:02,051 --> 00:05:03,177
They're in the safe.
It's that way.

84
00:05:11,977 --> 00:05:13,020
They loaded?

85
00:05:13,395 --> 00:05:14,939
- Just the nine.
- Let me see.

86
00:05:29,703 --> 00:05:30,746
Hmm.

87
00:05:32,164 --> 00:05:33,624
Well, it doesn't look
like it's been fired.

88
00:05:36,043 --> 00:05:37,169
Here's the thing, Cody.

89
00:05:37,586 --> 00:05:40,339
Sandra wasn't coming home
when she was killed, was she?

90
00:05:40,464 --> 00:05:41,674
She was leaving.

91
00:05:41,966 --> 00:05:44,260
She was just about to walk
out the door when...

92
00:05:44,593 --> 00:05:45,636
you got home.

93
00:05:46,095 --> 00:05:47,429
She was leaving you.

94
00:05:48,681 --> 00:05:50,474
No!
We've never been happier.

95
00:05:50,724 --> 00:05:52,560
She packed her suitcases

96
00:05:52,685 --> 00:05:56,230
left the engagement ring
on the table like you were nothing.

97
00:05:57,314 --> 00:05:59,275
But you're Cody Benbow,
for God's sakes.

98
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:01,193
- No one can treat you like that.
- No.

99
00:06:01,902 --> 00:06:04,238
And you got mad,
and you killed her.

100
00:06:05,823 --> 00:06:07,741
Then you had to unpack
her suitcase

101
00:06:07,867 --> 00:06:09,910
put her clothes
and her makeup away.

102
00:06:10,035 --> 00:06:11,704
But you did it poorly.

103
00:06:12,246 --> 00:06:14,081
Like a man putting away
a woman's things.

104
00:06:14,206 --> 00:06:16,709
Are you serious? That's your proof?
That--that her closet's messy?

105
00:06:17,918 --> 00:06:20,629
Yeah, you're right.
It's not really much, is it?

106
00:06:20,754 --> 00:06:22,006
It's not proof.

107
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:25,968
Proof would be finding
the jewelry and the cash

108
00:06:26,135 --> 00:06:27,428
that was taken from her.

109
00:06:27,887 --> 00:06:29,305
But a sensible man like you

110
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:31,599
wouldn't actually throw
that money away, would they?

111
00:06:32,182 --> 00:06:33,267
Even if they had the time.

112
00:06:33,392 --> 00:06:36,228
A sensible man would
hide it someplace

113
00:06:36,353 --> 00:06:38,105
that no one would think
to look.

114
00:06:38,272 --> 00:06:41,442
Someplace close,
like the garden.

115
00:06:42,902 --> 00:06:44,945
You buried it in the garden,
didn't you?

116
00:06:45,404 --> 00:06:48,574
You shut your mouth now!
Shut your mouth.

117
00:06:49,450 --> 00:06:51,201
I'll take that as a yes.

118
00:06:56,957 --> 00:06:59,043
- Oh, no!
- "Gun! He's got a gun!"

119
00:06:59,293 --> 00:07:02,254
Drop it! Don't be stupid!

120
00:07:03,297 --> 00:07:04,798
Back off, or he's dead.

121
00:07:10,930 --> 00:07:12,765
Okay, everyone just relax.

122
00:07:13,098 --> 00:07:15,726
There is no need for violence.

123
00:07:16,268 --> 00:07:19,813
The gun doesn't work.
I put peanuts in the chamber.

124
00:07:22,733 --> 00:07:24,777
So just come on down
and arrest him.

125
00:07:27,363 --> 00:07:30,032
- Drop it!
- Don't move!

126
00:07:31,241 --> 00:07:32,493
Keep your hands
where we can see them!

127
00:07:32,618 --> 00:07:33,661
Don't move!

128
00:07:41,752 --> 00:07:44,922
"We have an officer down, we need
a Medevac to this location ASAP."

129
00:07:45,047 --> 00:07:46,090
Secure his weapon.

130
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:47,883
I'm sorry.

131
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:12,823
Here he comes.

132
00:08:14,032 --> 00:08:15,075
Oh, crap.

133
00:08:15,742 --> 00:08:16,827
Just act naturally.

134
00:08:17,536 --> 00:08:19,663
- Breathe.
- I am breathing.

135
00:08:19,955 --> 00:08:20,998
Have you gone mad?

136
00:08:22,457 --> 00:08:25,168
{\an8}A-are you insane?
It-it's a serious question.

137
00:08:25,293 --> 00:08:26,670
Are you asking her
or are you asking me?

138
00:08:26,795 --> 00:08:28,839
'Cause speaking for myself,
the answer is no.

139
00:08:28,964 --> 00:08:31,508
{\an8}Lisbon--sane, not sane...

140
00:08:32,134 --> 00:08:35,262
{\an8}Sir, I fully recognize
that I failed in my duty

141
00:08:35,387 --> 00:08:38,223
{\an8}- to oversee Jane's behavior.
- Oh, stop. You did nothing wrong.

142
00:08:38,348 --> 00:08:40,559
{\an8}Agent Lisbon did wrong
when she failed

143
00:08:40,684 --> 00:08:42,644
to stop you furnishing guns
to a man

144
00:08:42,769 --> 00:08:45,939
who used those guns
to mow down a Sac P.D. officer.

145
00:08:46,606 --> 00:08:48,525
{\an8}It was a good plan
that went awry.

146
00:08:49,067 --> 00:08:51,361
I said "Sorry."
She's not responsible.

147
00:08:51,486 --> 00:08:52,821
That's her job.

148
00:08:53,655 --> 00:08:55,323
Being responsible.

149
00:08:55,449 --> 00:08:58,702
{\an8}- I am responsible.
- Sac P.D. brass wants your blood.

150
00:08:59,703 --> 00:09:01,830
So the question is,
should I suspend the both of you

151
00:09:01,955 --> 00:09:03,540
- for a month or--
- Sir, that is not fair.

152
00:09:03,665 --> 00:09:06,293
{\an8}Allow me to complete
my thought.

153
00:09:07,711 --> 00:09:09,379
{\an8}Should I suspend you

154
00:09:09,755 --> 00:09:12,299
{\an8}or should I just send you
on an outta-town case

155
00:09:12,424 --> 00:09:14,301
{\an8}far away from the Sac P.D.?

156
00:09:15,427 --> 00:09:16,511
The second option.

157
00:09:17,304 --> 00:09:18,346
{\an8}Oh?

158
00:09:18,597 --> 00:09:20,390
{\an8}I-I thought you might
prefer suspension.

159
00:09:20,515 --> 00:09:22,809
It frees you up to focus
on Red John.

160
00:09:22,934 --> 00:09:25,062
{\an8}- I hear you're getting close.
- Oh, yeah?

161
00:09:26,730 --> 00:09:27,773
{\an8}Where'd you hear that?

162
00:09:28,231 --> 00:09:30,025
Well, you can't keep secrets
around here.

163
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:32,110
There's a buzz all around
the building.

164
00:09:32,527 --> 00:09:34,988
{\an8}Jane's getting close, or thinks he is.

165
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:36,323
{\an8}Well, are you?

166
00:09:36,448 --> 00:09:39,743
{\an8}Eh, we have some
fresh leads.

167
00:09:40,243 --> 00:09:41,953
{\an8}We're trying to keep them
a little quiet.

168
00:09:42,079 --> 00:09:43,789
{\an8}Oh, yeah. Mum's the word.

169
00:09:44,539 --> 00:09:46,249
{\an8}But if you do get close,
you will tell me first

170
00:09:46,374 --> 00:09:48,460
{\an8}'cause I need to be on the boat
when you catch that fish.

171
00:09:48,585 --> 00:09:51,213
{\an8}- Do we understand each other?
- Yes, sir.

172
00:09:56,635 --> 00:10:00,764
{\an8}I know that sometimes
I must appear calculating

173
00:10:00,889 --> 00:10:04,184
{\an8}even duplicitous to you

174
00:10:04,351 --> 00:10:05,769
but please understand

175
00:10:05,894 --> 00:10:07,813
{\an8}it--it comes
with the job description.

176
00:10:07,938 --> 00:10:10,107
{\an8}I am many things
to many people.

177
00:10:14,027 --> 00:10:15,195
We understand.

178
00:10:15,779 --> 00:10:16,822
In the meantime...

179
00:10:19,741 --> 00:10:20,784
get outta town.

180
00:10:23,328 --> 00:10:24,663
Way outta town.

181
00:10:35,382 --> 00:10:36,800
{\an8}Are you thinking
what I'm thinking?

182
00:10:37,467 --> 00:10:38,510
No.

183
00:10:39,344 --> 00:10:40,971
{\an8}You're thinking
that if Bertram is Red John

184
00:10:41,096 --> 00:10:43,682
{\an8}he sent us out to the middle
of nowhere for a reason.

185
00:10:44,266 --> 00:10:47,060
{\an8}To get us out of the way
for when he makes his next move.

186
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:49,563
{\an8}I am thinking about...

187
00:10:50,438 --> 00:10:51,481
{\an8}trains.

188
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:55,110
{\an8}So what do we do?

189
00:10:55,569 --> 00:10:57,654
{\an8}- I don't know.
- Well, we have to do something.

190
00:10:57,779 --> 00:11:00,365
{\an8}Arrest them all, release
the names publicly--something.

191
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:02,701
Well, maybe that's what
Red John wants us to do.

192
00:11:03,243 --> 00:11:04,744
We could at least keep Bertram

193
00:11:04,870 --> 00:11:06,997
{\an8}and the other seven suspects
under surveillance

194
00:11:07,122 --> 00:11:09,583
{\an8}if the rest of the team
was involved, if they knew.

195
00:11:09,708 --> 00:11:12,878
No, no. We tell no one
about the list. No one.

196
00:11:13,044 --> 00:11:14,754
Well, if Red John knows
about the list already--

197
00:11:14,880 --> 00:11:17,674
Anyone else that knows
about the list will be in danger.

198
00:11:18,466 --> 00:11:19,634
We tell no one.

199
00:11:21,136 --> 00:11:22,804
I shouldn't have even told you.

200
00:11:49,539 --> 00:11:51,791
Kinda beautiful and weird, huh?

201
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:53,627
All the flowers.

202
00:11:54,377 --> 00:11:55,629
Hey, Partridge.

203
00:11:58,757 --> 00:12:00,008
How long's the body
been here?

204
00:12:00,217 --> 00:12:02,135
About 18 months, probably.
Uh...

205
00:12:02,594 --> 00:12:04,888
We think he was a large male,
early 40s, maybe.

206
00:12:05,013 --> 00:12:06,056
No teeth, no fingers.

207
00:12:06,181 --> 00:12:08,516
Probably removed by whoever brought him
here to prevent identification.

208
00:12:08,642 --> 00:12:10,477
But I bet if you cross-reference
that frat ring--

209
00:12:10,602 --> 00:12:11,937
That's what she's doing.

210
00:12:12,562 --> 00:12:14,105
Got you. Yeah.

211
00:12:15,273 --> 00:12:17,567
"Hey, boss.
Brooke Yardley."

212
00:12:17,692 --> 00:12:21,112
He was a Los Angeles
real estate developer, 46.

213
00:12:21,238 --> 00:12:24,074
His wife reported him missing
in August of 2011.

214
00:12:24,407 --> 00:12:27,118
I-I want you to run
a full profile on this guy ASAP.

215
00:12:27,244 --> 00:12:28,286
Will do.

216
00:12:29,579 --> 00:12:31,039
Hello, Mr. Yardley.

217
00:12:32,749 --> 00:12:34,876
Who did this to ya?
Hmm?

218
00:12:36,711 --> 00:12:37,963
How long until you're done
with forensics

219
00:12:38,088 --> 00:12:39,589
and you pull this guy
outta here?

220
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:43,343
Delicate job. We'll be outta here
by tomorrow morning, probably.

221
00:12:47,055 --> 00:12:48,098
Something wrong?

222
00:12:48,682 --> 00:12:49,724
Wrong?

223
00:12:50,016 --> 00:12:51,518
You've been really sullen
with me recently

224
00:12:51,643 --> 00:12:53,019
and now you're staring
at me strangely

225
00:12:53,144 --> 00:12:54,437
and I don't know why.

226
00:13:01,569 --> 00:13:02,862
You truly don't know?

227
00:13:05,573 --> 00:13:06,616
No.

228
00:13:09,619 --> 00:13:10,662
Hmm.

229
00:13:19,504 --> 00:13:21,923
Yardley was a wheeler-dealer
salesman type.

230
00:13:22,048 --> 00:13:23,883
No criminal record,
but he had his share

231
00:13:24,009 --> 00:13:25,510
of law and tax problems.

232
00:13:25,635 --> 00:13:26,720
Bankrupt twice.

233
00:13:27,137 --> 00:13:28,972
"His car was found abandoned
in Los Angeles"

234
00:13:29,097 --> 00:13:30,724
"a couple weeks
after his disappearance."

235
00:13:30,849 --> 00:13:33,435
- Any standout enemies?
- "Not off his paper trail."

236
00:13:33,768 --> 00:13:35,395
Cho and Rigsby are driving
to L.A. in the morning

237
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:36,938
to talk to the wife and son.

238
00:13:37,314 --> 00:13:38,982
All right. Jane and I need
to stay out here

239
00:13:39,107 --> 00:13:41,234
near the crime scene
till the body's dug up.

240
00:13:41,401 --> 00:13:43,320
We'll give you a call
when we find someplace to stay.

241
00:13:43,445 --> 00:13:44,487
"Sure."

242
00:13:46,323 --> 00:13:47,866
What's your take on Partridge?

243
00:13:48,158 --> 00:13:50,577
If he is Red John,
he's a very good actor.

244
00:13:50,952 --> 00:13:52,454
He would have to be,
wouldn't he?

245
00:13:55,457 --> 00:13:56,583
Wait. Slow down.

246
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:00,545
Let's turn here.
This looks interesting.

247
00:14:21,608 --> 00:14:23,401
- Thanks, hon.
- See you tomorrow, Sonya.

248
00:14:24,736 --> 00:14:25,945
Hey, folks.

249
00:14:29,115 --> 00:14:31,076
- And what can I get you?
- Tea, please.

250
00:14:31,284 --> 00:14:32,744
Oolong, preferably.

251
00:14:33,286 --> 00:14:35,705
Uh... Well... Just tea.

252
00:14:35,830 --> 00:14:38,041
Some loosely scrambled eggs
on rye toast.

253
00:14:38,166 --> 00:14:39,584
One strip of bacon

254
00:14:39,918 --> 00:14:42,462
- and one sausage, thanks.
- Okay.

255
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:46,633
- Coffee and muffin.
- Okay. Coming right up.

256
00:14:48,051 --> 00:14:49,886
You've heard
about the dead guy, I guess.

257
00:14:51,721 --> 00:14:54,182
Yeah, sure.
Everybody has.

258
00:14:54,682 --> 00:14:57,018
We're with the CBI.
We're investigating the case.

259
00:14:57,143 --> 00:14:58,853
Yeah. Sorry, I--
I didn't mean to scare you.

260
00:14:58,978 --> 00:15:01,272
Oh, no, no. I--
I'm not scared.

261
00:15:01,398 --> 00:15:04,275
I just... I've had
a bad feeling forever

262
00:15:04,401 --> 00:15:06,194
like I knew there was something
evil out there.

263
00:15:06,319 --> 00:15:07,529
I knew it.

264
00:15:08,446 --> 00:15:09,489
Didn't I, Hec?

265
00:15:10,323 --> 00:15:11,449
Uh-huh.

266
00:15:12,075 --> 00:15:13,159
Huh.

267
00:15:13,618 --> 00:15:15,120
Hmm, come to think...

268
00:15:15,662 --> 00:15:17,956
that I'm right?
My gosh.

269
00:15:18,289 --> 00:15:20,708
I mean, I always knew
I had a psychic gift

270
00:15:20,834 --> 00:15:22,377
but this is damn spooky.

271
00:15:22,502 --> 00:15:25,505
Psychic gift?
Excellent.

272
00:15:26,589 --> 00:15:29,551
Tell me, uh, will I enjoy
your eggs?

273
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:31,845
You betcha.

274
00:15:34,389 --> 00:15:35,432
We'll see.

275
00:15:40,812 --> 00:15:43,481
You're right, Sonya.
These are enjoyable eggs.

276
00:15:43,982 --> 00:15:45,942
Room for improvement,
but very enjoyable.

277
00:15:46,693 --> 00:15:47,735
Good.

278
00:15:50,238 --> 00:15:51,448
What are we doing, Jane?

279
00:15:51,948 --> 00:15:54,033
I'm eating eggs.
You're staring at a dry muffin.

280
00:15:54,159 --> 00:15:56,578
- We need a plan.
- I've been thinking about that.

281
00:15:56,703 --> 00:15:58,997
In the meantime, Red John
is gonna kill again.

282
00:15:59,122 --> 00:16:00,290
Yeah, probably.

283
00:16:01,958 --> 00:16:03,877
Jane, I'm scared.

284
00:16:04,043 --> 00:16:06,796
- He's not psychic.
- Well, that's not what scares me.

285
00:16:06,921 --> 00:16:09,674
- Well, no, that scares me, too.
- There's no such thing.

286
00:16:10,216 --> 00:16:13,178
I'm scared because I've never seen you
like this before.

287
00:16:13,303 --> 00:16:15,013
It's like you don't know
what to do next.

288
00:16:15,138 --> 00:16:17,348
Next?
Well, pecan pie.

289
00:16:17,474 --> 00:16:19,851
- Funny. Funny. I'm serious.
- So am I.

290
00:16:20,101 --> 00:16:21,394
- We need help.
- No.

291
00:16:52,008 --> 00:16:53,593
- Hey, boss.
- "Hey, we need to talk."

292
00:16:53,718 --> 00:16:54,761
"Are you alone?"

293
00:16:55,345 --> 00:16:58,348
Y-yeah. Well, I mean,
Wayne's with me.

294
00:16:58,473 --> 00:17:00,266
Call me back as soon
as you're on your own.

295
00:17:02,143 --> 00:17:03,895
Hello? Hello?

296
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:08,191
Lost her.
Bad signal in the desert.

297
00:17:09,025 --> 00:17:10,818
- She'll call right back.
- Yep.

298
00:17:15,907 --> 00:17:17,325
- Give me a moment, huh?
- Sure.

299
00:17:18,826 --> 00:17:20,453
- You okay?
- I'm fine.

300
00:17:31,339 --> 00:17:34,425
"You cannot tell anyone
what I'm about to tell you."

301
00:17:34,926 --> 00:17:35,969
Okay.

302
00:17:36,177 --> 00:17:39,013
Jane has worked out that
Red John is one of seven men.

303
00:17:39,514 --> 00:17:42,642
But Red John also knows
the seven names on Jane's list.

304
00:17:42,934 --> 00:17:44,727
- How?
- "We don't know."

305
00:17:44,852 --> 00:17:46,729
It's like he's read Jane's mind

306
00:17:47,105 --> 00:17:49,440
which has made Jane
paranoid, understandably.

307
00:17:50,108 --> 00:17:52,860
"He doesn't want anyone
to know about the list of names."

308
00:17:52,986 --> 00:17:55,071
"He doesn't know I'm talking
to you right now."

309
00:17:55,488 --> 00:17:57,448
He thinks whoever knows
about the names is in danger

310
00:17:57,574 --> 00:17:58,908
and he's probably right.

311
00:17:59,200 --> 00:18:02,245
But I have a strong feeling
we need to do something

312
00:18:02,370 --> 00:18:03,538
and I need your help.

313
00:18:03,705 --> 00:18:06,374
- "Do you want me to continue?"
- Yes.

314
00:18:06,624 --> 00:18:07,667
"Thank you."

315
00:18:08,126 --> 00:18:09,877
I'm gonna give you
the seven names

316
00:18:10,003 --> 00:18:11,838
and I need you to put
a G.P.S. bug

317
00:18:11,963 --> 00:18:13,548
on each of their cell phones.

318
00:18:13,673 --> 00:18:16,217
But you need to do it secretly.
Tell no one.

319
00:18:16,801 --> 00:18:18,511
- Can you do that?
- Yes, I can

320
00:18:18,636 --> 00:18:20,138
but when are you gonna tell Jane?

321
00:18:20,346 --> 00:18:21,723
When the time is right.

322
00:18:21,889 --> 00:18:24,475
Okay, I guess. Um...

323
00:18:24,892 --> 00:18:26,477
Let me get a pen and paper.

324
00:18:26,603 --> 00:18:28,396
No, I don't want you
to write anything down.

325
00:18:37,155 --> 00:18:40,033
- Everything okay?
- Sure, of course. I, um...

326
00:18:40,575 --> 00:18:41,784
spoke to Lisbon.

327
00:18:42,368 --> 00:18:43,578
I have work to do.

328
00:18:43,953 --> 00:18:45,747
Work? Now?

329
00:18:46,456 --> 00:18:48,708
- What's so urgent?
- Oh...

330
00:18:48,833 --> 00:18:51,419
You know Lisbon.
No sense of time.

331
00:18:52,086 --> 00:18:53,796
You go on home.
I'll see you there.

332
00:18:55,632 --> 00:18:56,924
What's going on, Grace?

333
00:18:57,550 --> 00:18:58,760
Nothing.

334
00:19:24,116 --> 00:19:25,785
- Something wrong?
- No.

335
00:19:30,581 --> 00:19:31,624
What do you mean?

336
00:19:32,208 --> 00:19:33,292
You're acting weird

337
00:19:33,751 --> 00:19:35,252
like you're keeping something
from me.

338
00:19:35,878 --> 00:19:37,463
What?
No, dude.

339
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:39,507
What would I be keeping
from you?

340
00:19:41,676 --> 00:19:43,969
- You're acting weird.
- Weird, like how?

341
00:19:46,263 --> 00:19:47,682
Like you can't look me
in the eye.

342
00:19:48,974 --> 00:19:50,226
Yes, I can.

343
00:19:50,601 --> 00:19:52,937
- You happy now?
- No. What's going on, Wayne?

344
00:19:56,691 --> 00:19:57,942
Am I intruding?

345
00:19:58,484 --> 00:20:00,277
Are you sure it's my husband?

346
00:20:00,528 --> 00:20:02,488
- Mrs. Yardley, we--
- Madison.

347
00:20:03,155 --> 00:20:04,865
Madison. I'm sorry.

348
00:20:04,990 --> 00:20:06,242
We're dealing
with a skeleton here

349
00:20:06,367 --> 00:20:09,286
so we can't be sure of ID
until DNA test results are done.

350
00:20:09,412 --> 00:20:10,955
But we think so, yes.

351
00:20:11,163 --> 00:20:12,373
Oh, thank God.

352
00:20:15,543 --> 00:20:17,545
Well, don't get me wrong
I loved Brooke very much

353
00:20:17,670 --> 00:20:21,006
but I've known in my heart
he's been dead for some time now.

354
00:20:21,132 --> 00:20:23,008
He would never have run away
without a word.

355
00:20:23,676 --> 00:20:25,594
It's just good
to finally know the truth.

356
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:28,305
I've been in the most
hellish limbo.

357
00:20:28,472 --> 00:20:30,558
Records indicate he had started
divorce proceedings.

358
00:20:30,683 --> 00:20:33,144
Yes. It was a mutual decision.

359
00:20:33,978 --> 00:20:36,814
You can love someone very much
and not be able to live with them.

360
00:20:37,022 --> 00:20:38,065
What was the problem?

361
00:20:38,315 --> 00:20:41,485
Oh, Brooke was a dog.
Pathological womanizer.

362
00:20:41,861 --> 00:20:44,405
I knew it when I married him.
I'm his third wife. Duh.

363
00:20:44,530 --> 00:20:46,699
But he was so charming
and witty and handsome.

364
00:20:46,824 --> 00:20:47,950
He cheated on you.

365
00:20:49,076 --> 00:20:50,828
Cheating would involve deception.

366
00:20:51,245 --> 00:20:52,621
He never tried to deceive me.

367
00:20:52,747 --> 00:20:54,623
Some would call that good motive
for killing him.

368
00:20:54,749 --> 00:20:55,958
Well, I didn't.

369
00:20:58,502 --> 00:20:59,837
But I know who did
kill him

370
00:21:00,212 --> 00:21:01,255
if you wanna know.

371
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:03,299
I told the police two years ago,
but they weren't interested.

372
00:21:03,424 --> 00:21:04,967
"No body, no crime",
they said.

373
00:21:05,843 --> 00:21:07,303
So you know who killed him.

374
00:21:07,678 --> 00:21:09,930
A man called Arek Green.

375
00:21:10,055 --> 00:21:11,974
He owns nightclubs
across the border.

376
00:21:12,099 --> 00:21:14,185
Brooke went to a meeting
with Arek in Mexicali

377
00:21:14,310 --> 00:21:16,604
and that was the last anyone
ever saw Brooke.

378
00:21:16,854 --> 00:21:19,190
And why would Mr. Green have reason
to kill your husband?

379
00:21:19,648 --> 00:21:21,859
Arek owed Brooke something
around $100,000

380
00:21:21,984 --> 00:21:23,652
on a property deal
and didn't wanna pay.

381
00:21:23,778 --> 00:21:24,945
Brooke was going to sue him.

382
00:21:25,321 --> 00:21:27,573
That's motive, isn't it?

383
00:21:32,286 --> 00:21:35,581
This is my stepson Gavin.
He lives here also.

384
00:21:37,291 --> 00:21:38,876
These are police of some kind.

385
00:21:39,126 --> 00:21:40,836
They found your father's body.

386
00:21:42,797 --> 00:21:43,839
Oh.

387
00:21:45,257 --> 00:21:47,635
Really? Where?

388
00:21:48,969 --> 00:21:50,179
Buried in the desert.

389
00:21:50,679 --> 00:21:52,014
How squalid.

390
00:21:52,473 --> 00:21:55,100
- Murder, was it?
- That's our supposition, yes.

391
00:21:56,393 --> 00:21:58,312
Well, I'd take a good, long look
at this one.

392
00:21:58,729 --> 00:22:00,731
She's perfectly capable of murder.

393
00:22:01,023 --> 00:22:04,193
Too lazy, perhaps, but she could've
summoned a burst of energy.

394
00:22:04,318 --> 00:22:07,279
She did so despise
my poor, dear father.

395
00:22:07,404 --> 00:22:10,699
You are a bitter, twisted
little fantasist.

396
00:22:11,116 --> 00:22:13,202
We had our battles,
but I loved that man.

397
00:22:13,327 --> 00:22:14,954
Oh! Dishonest crap.

398
00:22:15,079 --> 00:22:17,873
These are professional
policemen, Madison.

399
00:22:17,998 --> 00:22:19,708
They can see right through you.

400
00:22:19,834 --> 00:22:22,169
Okay.
Returning to Arek Green...

401
00:22:22,294 --> 00:22:24,171
When was this meeting
in Mexicali, exactly?

402
00:22:24,296 --> 00:22:27,716
July 10, two years ago.
I apologize for my stepson.

403
00:22:27,925 --> 00:22:30,010
When Brooke is officially dead,
his will is enforceable

404
00:22:30,135 --> 00:22:32,680
and Gavin will have to leave
my house.

405
00:22:32,805 --> 00:22:34,181
Finally.
Hence the anger.

406
00:22:34,306 --> 00:22:35,933
Oh, don't think for a second
I won't dispute

407
00:22:36,058 --> 00:22:37,226
every inch of that will.

408
00:22:37,351 --> 00:22:39,478
Oh, I know you will.
You're such a child.

409
00:22:39,728 --> 00:22:41,897
God, I can't believe I ever let you
have sex with me.

410
00:22:42,439 --> 00:22:44,650
Please.
You were practically begging me.

411
00:22:46,318 --> 00:22:48,529
I am so sorry.
I blame his mother.

412
00:22:48,654 --> 00:22:49,989
Now she was a piece of work.

413
00:22:50,114 --> 00:22:53,158
Don't you say a word about my mother
you conniving little slut.

414
00:22:53,284 --> 00:22:55,995
- Hey, folks--
- I will talk about who the hell I like.

415
00:22:56,120 --> 00:22:58,163
Your mother
was a cold-hearted bitch!

416
00:22:58,289 --> 00:23:01,417
Okay. Whoa, whoa, whoa!
Let's just take a breath.

417
00:23:06,463 --> 00:23:09,383
Low heat, then a little water.

418
00:23:09,758 --> 00:23:11,844
Keeps the eggs
nice and loose.

419
00:23:12,845 --> 00:23:14,889
I've been cooking eggs
for 20 years.

420
00:23:15,055 --> 00:23:17,850
Oh, so your cup's full.
Can't learn any more, huh?

421
00:23:19,143 --> 00:23:21,770
- Not about eggs.
- Okay.

422
00:23:23,564 --> 00:23:24,607
"Bon appétit."

423
00:23:34,491 --> 00:23:35,534
Damn.

424
00:23:39,830 --> 00:23:40,873
Jane.

425
00:23:42,041 --> 00:23:43,208
Come here?

426
00:23:49,924 --> 00:23:53,510
So both Yardley's son and wife
are still possible suspects.

427
00:23:53,636 --> 00:23:56,055
But the wife says the last time
she saw Yardley

428
00:23:56,180 --> 00:23:57,556
he was heading down
to Mexicali

429
00:23:57,681 --> 00:23:59,892
to demand money outta
some shady businessman--

430
00:24:00,225 --> 00:24:01,810
Guy named Arek Green.

431
00:24:02,019 --> 00:24:04,772
He's got a criminal record,
underworld connections

432
00:24:05,064 --> 00:24:07,942
And right now, we're about
a hundred miles from Mexicali.

433
00:24:08,233 --> 00:24:09,318
Sounds like a lead.

434
00:24:09,443 --> 00:24:11,612
Yeah, I'm thinking we should go
and talk to this guy Arek Green.

435
00:24:12,446 --> 00:24:14,323
Yeah, I-I'm gonna stay here.
You go.

436
00:24:14,448 --> 00:24:15,866
You can manage
on your own, right?

437
00:24:15,991 --> 00:24:18,410
- Of course I can, but--
- Well, I-I like the quiet here.

438
00:24:18,869 --> 00:24:20,204
The desert helps me think.

439
00:24:20,537 --> 00:24:21,872
You know what?
I'll stay.

440
00:24:22,122 --> 00:24:24,124
Cho and Rigsby can go
to Mexicali.

441
00:24:24,249 --> 00:24:27,086
- Don't trust me alone, huh?
- I never have.

442
00:24:28,462 --> 00:24:30,589
I'm gonna go and run
some calls.

443
00:24:32,174 --> 00:24:33,217
Sure.

444
00:24:44,436 --> 00:24:46,063
Boss, the wires are up.

445
00:24:46,397 --> 00:24:47,815
- All of them?
- Yep.

446
00:24:47,940 --> 00:24:51,026
G.P.S. and audio on all seven
Red John suspects.

447
00:24:51,485 --> 00:24:54,613
Good work.
Okay, we watch them like hawks.

448
00:24:54,738 --> 00:24:57,449
We log everywhere they go, 24/7.

449
00:24:57,908 --> 00:25:00,411
- Will do.
- And remember, do not--

450
00:25:00,536 --> 00:25:02,663
I-I know. Tell nobody.

451
00:25:20,222 --> 00:25:23,225
That's a shame.
Yardley was good people.

452
00:25:24,685 --> 00:25:27,187
Mr. Green, we're told you and Yardley
were in a business dispute.

453
00:25:28,022 --> 00:25:29,481
Yeah, I mean, you know...

454
00:25:29,857 --> 00:25:30,899
Yard loved drama.

455
00:25:31,525 --> 00:25:33,944
But we're old friends
ten years now--

456
00:25:34,069 --> 00:25:35,779
and it was nothing.

457
00:25:35,904 --> 00:25:37,948
It seems like you were the last person
to see him alive.

458
00:25:38,073 --> 00:25:39,783
And you owed him $100,000.

459
00:25:40,492 --> 00:25:43,037
Which I gave to him. In cash.

460
00:25:43,370 --> 00:25:45,289
Last time I saw him,
he was a happy man.

461
00:25:46,206 --> 00:25:48,292
- Very happy.
- You get a receipt?

462
00:25:48,459 --> 00:25:51,295
You know, it wasn't
that kind of transaction.

463
00:25:51,545 --> 00:25:53,088
Uh-huh. Go on.

464
00:25:53,505 --> 00:25:55,841
Look, I pay my debts.

465
00:25:56,175 --> 00:25:58,886
I heard he vanished, I thought he went
somewhere like Brazil or something

466
00:25:59,011 --> 00:26:01,138
so he didn't have to share
the money with the old lady.

467
00:26:01,513 --> 00:26:02,806
Now you're telling me he's dead.

468
00:26:03,390 --> 00:26:05,267
I gotta assume
the loopy bitch did it.

469
00:26:05,392 --> 00:26:06,935
Or that wacko son.

470
00:26:09,688 --> 00:26:10,814
Have you met those two?

471
00:26:12,149 --> 00:26:13,233
Oh, you did?

472
00:26:14,818 --> 00:26:15,861
So you know.

473
00:26:18,739 --> 00:26:21,116
So you think
it's either the greedy wife

474
00:26:21,241 --> 00:26:23,869
the angry son,
or the shady business partner?

475
00:26:25,370 --> 00:26:26,413
Looks that way.

476
00:26:27,206 --> 00:26:30,042
I think we should get 'em out here
take a look at 'em.

477
00:26:30,876 --> 00:26:32,920
How are we gonna do that,
arrest them all?

478
00:26:33,504 --> 00:26:35,339
Well, tell them we're gonna
find out

479
00:26:35,464 --> 00:26:37,466
where the $100,000 cash is.
They'll come.

480
00:26:37,591 --> 00:26:39,384
Well, what if there
was no 100 grand in cash?

481
00:26:39,510 --> 00:26:41,720
Well, if there wasn't, the wife
and the son don't know it

482
00:26:41,845 --> 00:26:44,306
and the shady business partner
has to pretend there was, right?

483
00:26:44,807 --> 00:26:46,141
- I guess.
- Yeah.

484
00:27:12,543 --> 00:27:13,669
They're here. Now what?

485
00:27:13,794 --> 00:27:15,963
I think a simple seance
should do the trick.

486
00:27:16,421 --> 00:27:18,340
- Seriously?
- Seriously.

487
00:27:19,174 --> 00:27:20,592
Welcome. Madison.

488
00:27:22,302 --> 00:27:25,139
Gavin. My name is Patrick Jane.
This is Agent Lisbon.

489
00:27:25,264 --> 00:27:27,266
Thanks for coming all the way
out here to help us.

490
00:27:27,474 --> 00:27:28,642
Hey, Van Pelt.

491
00:27:29,560 --> 00:27:30,602
Hey, Jane.

492
00:27:30,853 --> 00:27:33,856
So where's the money you promised?

493
00:27:34,773 --> 00:27:35,816
Have a seat.

494
00:27:36,191 --> 00:27:37,526
Um, try the eggs.

495
00:27:37,943 --> 00:27:40,863
All will be revealed
in very good time.

496
00:27:40,988 --> 00:27:43,657
I just need to have a quick word
with my colleagues.

497
00:27:52,624 --> 00:27:53,917
Could you give us a minute, please?

498
00:27:57,462 --> 00:27:59,673
- What?
- She knows about the list.

499
00:28:01,508 --> 00:28:04,052
Yes, but only Grace.
Nobody else.

500
00:28:04,469 --> 00:28:06,680
Now we can keep an eye
on the suspects.

501
00:28:07,097 --> 00:28:08,515
You bugged their phones.

502
00:28:08,974 --> 00:28:10,017
Yes.

503
00:28:10,309 --> 00:28:13,645
Red John will know what you've done
and he will use it against us.

504
00:28:13,770 --> 00:28:16,064
He will lead you
wherever he wants to.

505
00:28:16,190 --> 00:28:19,484
I am not naive.
We'll act with that possibility in mind.

506
00:28:19,610 --> 00:28:22,362
But it's better to have the information
available to us.

507
00:28:22,487 --> 00:28:24,823
- It's better than doing nothing at all.
- No, no, no.

508
00:28:24,948 --> 00:28:27,534
I-I told you--
Tell no one! I told you!

509
00:28:27,659 --> 00:28:30,162
You told me?
You are not my boss.

510
00:28:30,287 --> 00:28:32,372
This is my case.
Red John is my case.

511
00:28:32,497 --> 00:28:33,540
You work for me.

512
00:28:33,665 --> 00:28:35,250
Oh, come on, please,
Lisbon, don't be childish.

513
00:28:35,375 --> 00:28:37,669
- Don't be arrogant!
- You are out of your depth.

514
00:28:37,794 --> 00:28:39,630
- You can't--
- You can't tell me what to do

515
00:28:39,755 --> 00:28:42,382
because I don't think
you know what you're doing yourself.

516
00:28:42,507 --> 00:28:44,843
I have to do what I have to do
to do my job.

517
00:28:45,219 --> 00:28:47,679
Now I'm going back to Sacramento
to keep an eye on the suspects.

518
00:28:47,804 --> 00:28:49,139
You can do whatever
you want.

519
00:28:50,307 --> 00:28:52,059
Grace, you're in charge
until Cho gets here.

520
00:28:52,184 --> 00:28:54,311
- After that, he's in charge.
- Okay, boss.

521
00:29:00,943 --> 00:29:01,985
Wait!

522
00:29:08,325 --> 00:29:11,954
Hey. Hey, Lisbon. Lisbon!

523
00:29:40,605 --> 00:29:43,608
Mr. Jane, agent Van Pelt,
this is Arek Green.

524
00:29:44,901 --> 00:29:46,111
Good. Nice to meet you,
Mr. Green.

525
00:29:46,236 --> 00:29:47,988
Why don't you go inside
the diner, take a seat?

526
00:29:48,113 --> 00:29:49,823
I just need to talk
to my colleagues.

527
00:29:57,580 --> 00:29:58,957
You told them about the list.

528
00:29:59,582 --> 00:30:00,625
No.

529
00:30:03,628 --> 00:30:04,754
I told Rigsby.

530
00:30:06,756 --> 00:30:08,758
Look, I had to.
He thought I was having an affair.

531
00:30:08,883 --> 00:30:10,135
She was acting weird.

532
00:30:15,098 --> 00:30:16,391
You told Cho.

533
00:30:16,516 --> 00:30:17,934
Well, he can't be the only member
of the team that doesn't know.

534
00:30:18,059 --> 00:30:19,394
Come on, that's not fair.

535
00:30:20,312 --> 00:30:21,479
I'm not gonna tell anybody.

536
00:30:21,730 --> 00:30:23,940
Lisbon was only doing
what she thought was best.

537
00:30:24,649 --> 00:30:26,067
You have to make up with her.

538
00:30:28,445 --> 00:30:29,487
Thanks.

539
00:30:40,999 --> 00:30:42,042
So what's the plan?

540
00:30:42,167 --> 00:30:43,585
Well, if anyone tries
to kill me, shoot 'em.

541
00:30:49,466 --> 00:30:51,760
Okay, hello, everybody.
My name is Patrick Jane.

542
00:30:51,885 --> 00:30:54,429
I'm with the CBI.
We're here to solve a murder.

543
00:30:55,597 --> 00:30:57,766
Uh, Sonya, will you help me?

544
00:30:58,016 --> 00:30:59,559
I need your psychic gifts.

545
00:31:00,060 --> 00:31:02,062
Me? Oh, no, no.
I don't think so.

546
00:31:02,187 --> 00:31:06,691
Yeah, please. Come take
a good look at these three people.

547
00:31:07,484 --> 00:31:09,944
They all had reasons to kill
Brooke Yardley.

548
00:31:10,070 --> 00:31:12,822
Are you kidding me?
What reason did I have?

549
00:31:12,947 --> 00:31:15,283
If you're innocent, hush, please.

550
00:31:16,159 --> 00:31:19,079
Uh, you knew that Yardley
was out there, didn't you?

551
00:31:19,204 --> 00:31:21,623
You heard him.
He spoke to you.

552
00:31:21,915 --> 00:31:24,125
I guess, in a way.

553
00:31:24,250 --> 00:31:27,087
Yardley can tell us
which one of these three

554
00:31:27,212 --> 00:31:29,172
is the guilty one,
just like he told you

555
00:31:29,297 --> 00:31:30,757
that he was out there.

556
00:31:32,050 --> 00:31:33,134
He can?

557
00:31:33,259 --> 00:31:36,346
- Uh, this is very amusing, but I'm...
- Quiet.

558
00:31:37,489 --> 00:31:39,032
Thank you, Cho.

559
00:31:41,743 --> 00:31:44,162
He spoke to your subconscious

560
00:31:44,287 --> 00:31:47,207
and we need to speak to him
in that way.

561
00:31:52,879 --> 00:31:54,047
This will do.

562
00:31:54,964 --> 00:31:57,884
Uh... Would you just put
your hand just like that?

563
00:31:58,218 --> 00:32:01,095
Just... relax.
Just like that. Okay.

564
00:32:01,930 --> 00:32:03,515
Now close your eyes.

565
00:32:05,350 --> 00:32:06,893
Think about Brooke Yardley.

566
00:32:07,936 --> 00:32:10,355
Ask him to tell us
what he wants to tell us.

567
00:32:11,272 --> 00:32:12,440
Think deeply.

568
00:32:13,233 --> 00:32:14,776
Okay, and when you're ready...

569
00:32:16,486 --> 00:32:17,946
let your hand fall.

570
00:32:22,700 --> 00:32:25,578
"L". Uh, Van Pelt, would you write
these down, please?

571
00:32:26,621 --> 00:32:28,248
Keep thinking about Yardley.

572
00:32:28,665 --> 00:32:30,166
What does he wanna say?

573
00:32:30,875 --> 00:32:32,210
And when you feel it...

574
00:32:33,169 --> 00:32:34,212
do it again.

575
00:32:35,463 --> 00:32:37,507
"U".
And again.

576
00:32:38,383 --> 00:32:39,551
"K".

577
00:32:41,386 --> 00:32:42,845
"E".

578
00:32:44,347 --> 00:32:45,598
"12"?

579
00:32:46,182 --> 00:32:47,559
"27".

580
00:32:49,269 --> 00:32:50,770
Luke 12:27?

581
00:32:53,314 --> 00:32:56,192
Is there anyone connected
to the case named Luke?

582
00:32:56,442 --> 00:32:58,903
Maybe it's the, uh, bible verse?

583
00:32:59,279 --> 00:33:02,198
Luke 12:27. Wh--what does--
what does that say?

584
00:33:02,323 --> 00:33:05,159
"Consider the lilies,
how they grow."

585
00:33:05,285 --> 00:33:08,246
- "They toil not..."
- "Neither do they spin."

586
00:33:10,331 --> 00:33:12,292
What does that have to do
with anything?

587
00:33:12,792 --> 00:33:13,835
I don't know.

588
00:33:15,587 --> 00:33:17,630
Wait a minute.
"How they grow".

589
00:33:18,298 --> 00:33:21,426
Yardley had lilies on his grave.
Well, flowers, anyway.

590
00:33:21,551 --> 00:33:24,137
Uh, not--not the normal kinda flowers
you'd see in the desert.

591
00:33:24,262 --> 00:33:26,014
And grass, for that matter.

592
00:33:26,889 --> 00:33:28,308
That's what he's trying
to tell us.

593
00:33:28,766 --> 00:33:31,311
How did those flowers
end up there?

594
00:33:32,145 --> 00:33:34,188
And--and how
did they get water?

595
00:33:35,607 --> 00:33:37,442
Well, there's only one
logical explanation.

596
00:33:37,567 --> 00:33:39,819
The body was in a block of ice.

597
00:33:39,944 --> 00:33:44,240
A block of ice that was carried
in a flat-bed truck

598
00:33:44,866 --> 00:33:48,161
that normally carries
garden supplies

599
00:33:48,328 --> 00:33:50,788
like flower and grass seeds.

600
00:33:51,289 --> 00:33:54,792
Seeds that stuck to a block of ice.

601
00:33:55,418 --> 00:33:57,337
- Clever.
- Yes, but...

602
00:33:57,462 --> 00:33:59,422
But ice melts very fast
in the desert

603
00:33:59,547 --> 00:34:02,842
so that block couldn't have been moved
very far from where it started.

604
00:34:03,217 --> 00:34:05,345
What we're looking
for is someplace

605
00:34:05,470 --> 00:34:07,013
close to the crime scene

606
00:34:07,388 --> 00:34:10,433
where they have a large
commercial freezer.

607
00:34:10,542 --> 00:34:12,460
- Like in a diner.
- Yes.

608
00:34:13,127 --> 00:34:15,463
But why would anyone
at the diner

609
00:34:15,588 --> 00:34:17,298
wanna kill Brooke Yardley?

610
00:34:18,258 --> 00:34:20,843
Yeah, I...
Hello.

611
00:34:21,052 --> 00:34:22,845
Well, unless, of course,
he was...

612
00:34:23,137 --> 00:34:25,348
flashing all that money
he had.

613
00:34:26,224 --> 00:34:28,142
And he was that kinda guy.

614
00:34:28,434 --> 00:34:31,729
You know, a showoff
and a ladies' man, right?

615
00:34:31,854 --> 00:34:32,897
Yes.

616
00:34:34,440 --> 00:34:36,442
I bet he hit on you, Sonya.
Yeah?

617
00:34:36,568 --> 00:34:38,611
Well, you said no, of course.

618
00:34:38,945 --> 00:34:40,572
Then he offered you money.

619
00:34:43,157 --> 00:34:45,618
"And you're a good woman
but everyone has their price."

620
00:34:48,830 --> 00:34:50,665
"Your boyfriend Hector
is the jealous type."

621
00:34:50,790 --> 00:34:54,210
"But Brooke was a big man
so Hec probably got Mac to help out."

622
00:35:00,967 --> 00:35:03,678
Then the three of you argued
over what to do next.

623
00:35:04,012 --> 00:35:06,848
Do you let Yardley go, or do you
kill him and take the money?

624
00:35:07,348 --> 00:35:10,018
Your biggest mistake--
you let him hear you.

625
00:35:11,311 --> 00:35:13,563
- "What are you doing?"
- "We have to kill him."

626
00:35:14,230 --> 00:35:15,398
"He knew he was gonna die"

627
00:35:15,523 --> 00:35:17,817
"so he made sure
his body was identifiable"

628
00:35:18,192 --> 00:35:20,069
"by swallowing his ring."

629
00:35:20,528 --> 00:35:24,949
- "Mac's right. We gotta kill him!"
- "No! Mac! No!"

630
00:35:29,120 --> 00:35:30,371
This is garbage.

631
00:35:30,538 --> 00:35:32,415
Fantasy.
You have no evidence.

632
00:35:32,540 --> 00:35:34,751
This from a man wearing a hat

633
00:35:34,876 --> 00:35:38,504
that says
"Landscaping and gardening supplies."

634
00:35:38,630 --> 00:35:41,132
There's plenty of other guys
that work at garden supply stores.

635
00:35:41,674 --> 00:35:43,968
And plenty of other diners
that got freezers.

636
00:35:44,093 --> 00:35:47,472
Yes, but this is the only one
with a fancy new sign on the highway.

637
00:35:47,597 --> 00:35:49,015
How much did that cost you?

638
00:35:57,607 --> 00:35:59,108
It-it was them!

639
00:35:59,317 --> 00:36:01,778
They killed him. I--
They tried to keep me quiet.

640
00:36:01,903 --> 00:36:03,863
- I had nothing to do with it.
- Sonya, shut up!

641
00:36:04,155 --> 00:36:05,323
Book 'em, guys.

642
00:36:18,125 --> 00:36:19,377
Not now, Jane.

643
00:36:20,795 --> 00:36:23,339
"This is Teresa Lisbon.
I can't take your call right now."

644
00:36:23,464 --> 00:36:25,091
"Please leave a message
after the beep."

645
00:36:26,300 --> 00:36:29,553
Uh, hi, Lisbon.
Um... Call me.

646
00:36:31,472 --> 00:36:34,517
Uh... I'm sorry we argued.
I just, uh...

647
00:36:34,725 --> 00:36:36,519
I realized, um...

648
00:36:37,436 --> 00:36:39,605
Yeah, just--just call me.
We'll chat.

649
00:36:49,990 --> 00:36:51,033
Lisbon.

650
00:36:51,158 --> 00:36:52,952
"We have an anonymous tip
directed to your unit."

651
00:36:53,077 --> 00:36:56,622
"A person or persons in distress
at 5570 West Huron."

652
00:36:56,747 --> 00:36:59,792
"At 5570 West Huron.
Okay, I got it."

653
00:37:06,424 --> 00:37:07,466
That's weird.

654
00:37:08,426 --> 00:37:09,969
Brett Partridge is there.

655
00:37:15,224 --> 00:37:16,809
This is Agent Lisbon
with the CBI.

656
00:37:16,934 --> 00:37:20,646
I need Sac P.D. to roll code
to 5570 West Huron.

657
00:37:20,771 --> 00:37:23,983
Immediate assistance,
5570 West Huron.

658
00:37:33,284 --> 00:37:36,036
Yeah, no, I understand. When do
you think they're gonna be here?

659
00:37:38,873 --> 00:37:39,915
All right.

660
00:37:42,126 --> 00:37:43,169
Thanks for nothing.

661
00:37:56,056 --> 00:37:57,099
Damn it.

662
00:38:42,978 --> 00:38:45,523
"This is Teresa Lisbon.
I can't take your call right now."

663
00:38:45,648 --> 00:38:47,358
"Please leave a message
after the beep."

664
00:38:48,359 --> 00:38:49,652
Where are you?

665
00:38:50,236 --> 00:38:51,362
Call me.

666
00:40:01,640 --> 00:40:02,683
Oh, my God.

667
00:40:03,392 --> 00:40:05,728
Partridge? Partridge?

668
00:40:09,481 --> 00:40:10,691
T...

669
00:40:11,692 --> 00:40:13,152
T... Tiger.

670
00:40:14,361 --> 00:40:15,654
Tiger.

671
00:40:38,510 --> 00:40:40,137
Uh, excuse me,
have you seen Lisbon?

672
00:40:40,262 --> 00:40:41,430
Nope. Haven't seen her.

673
00:40:42,014 --> 00:40:43,140
Ah, great.

674
00:40:46,435 --> 00:40:47,895
Lisbon, finally.

675
00:40:48,312 --> 00:40:51,231
"Sorry, Patrick. Teresa can't come
to the phone right now."

676
00:40:51,357 --> 00:40:53,108
"Can I take a message?"

677
00:40:57,489 --> 00:40:58,574
No?

678
00:41:00,861 --> 00:41:02,487
"Well, I'll tell her you called."

679
00:41:03,174 --> 00:41:04,383
Wait--

